<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>A 51-year-old right-handed writer with six months of worsening tremor of the writing hand on a background of milder shaking for several years. Please classify the tremor, exclude early Parkinson disease and other causes, and advise on treatment. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two to four weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What type of tremor is this, by the MDS consensus classification: essential tremor, an enhanced physiological tremor from alcohol or caffeine, or early Parkinson disease?</item><item>Is any imaging, such as brain MRI or dopamine transporter scanning, needed?</item><item>Do you advise propranolol, and at what dose and titration, or primidone instead?</item><item>What can he do to keep writing by hand, and is occupational therapy worthwhile?</item><item>How should his drinking, which briefly steadies the tremor, be addressed in the treatment plan?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Shakespeare is a 51-year-old playwright and poet who writes by hand first. For about three or four years he has noticed a slight shake when holding a full cup; over the past six months his handwriting has become larger, jagged, and tiring, and his signature is shaky. The tremor is worse when he is tired, after coffee, and the morning after an evening of drinking, and it eases for about an hour after a glass of wine. He has no rest tremor, slowness, stiffness, change in gait, voice, or smell, constipation, or dream enactment. He takes no medicines and has no asthma, heart block, or diabetes. He drinks about 24 standard drinks a week (AUDIT 12) without morning drinking or withdrawal; a brief intervention was given today with a goal of no more than 14 drinks a week and four on any day. He drinks four to five cups of coffee a day. He does not know of tremor in his family. On examination today: bilateral postural and kinetic hand tremor, right more than left, without rest tremor, bradykinesia, rigidity, or ataxia; pulse 80; blood pressure 134/84 mmHg. TSH, a metabolic panel, liver tests, and a blood count were drawn today [S]</paragraph><list><item>His hand shakes when he writes and when he signs his name, and his pages are getting harder to read. [S]</item><item>Wants to know whether this is the beginning of something worse, such as Parkinson disease. [S]</item><item>Wants to keep writing by hand. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>William Shakespeare was born in Stratford-upon-Avon in April 1564; the exact date of his birth is not recorded, although it is most often celebrated on 23 April. His baptism is recorded in the parish register of Holy Trinity Church on Wednesday 26 April 1564 (Julian; 6 May 1564, Gregorian), in Latin, as &apos;Gulielmus filius Johannes Shakspere&apos;. Baptisms typically took place within three days of birth. [D]</item><item>His father, John Shakespeare, was a glover who also traded in wool and lent money, served on the town council, and became high bailiff in 1568. Before William was born his parents had lost two infant daughters, Joan within the first few weeks of her life and Margaret a year after she was born; a younger sister, Anne, died at the age of eight. [D]</item><item>At eighteen he married Anne Hathaway of Shottery; their daughter Susanna was born six months after the marriage, followed by the twins Judith and Hamnet in 1585. Hamnet died at the age of eleven. [D]</item><item>His wife and children remained in Stratford while he worked in London, and in 1597 he bought New Place, a large house in the centre of the town, which was his family home. [D]</item></list><paragraph>6 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

